{"about":"da6f74bed07c54a4f0de22cdecdce71da65119ad40ce1aa00214ae8c81c9aeca","body":"The argument that we choose highly corroborated theories because they are the 'most informative target for future falsification' is internally inconsistent with scientific practice. Scientists do not subject their most trusted, well-corroborated theories to destructive testing more often than fringe hypotheses; rather, they use the corroborated theories as a stable foundation to test new, less corroborated ideas. If the goal were truly to maximize the speed of falsification, one should target the most robust theories first to knock them down, but science proceeds by tentatively accepting the robust ones. This suggests the preference is actually epistemic (believing the theory is likely true) rather than the methodological gamble the conjecture describes.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}